  "abstract": "We propose a reduction for a six-clique whose vertices have an identical\noutside neighborhood of order at most six in a biplanar graph. The reduction\nworks in the actual planar-layer embeddings and preserves every exterior\nedge. A local cofacial completion is triangle-free. When a private layer\nis connected, terminal stars determine plane contact trees, and a small\nwindow lemma chooses disjoint terminal groups on the boundary of a regular\nneighborhood. Keeping the cycles of the private layer supplies two disjoint\nrouting trees inside that surface. In a disconnected layer, a secondary\ncomponent on at most two vertices either can be deleted without changing\nterminal cofaciality or forces a terminal cycle with a compatible pair.\nThese alternatives coordinate the same identifications in the two layers.\nThe resulting graph has at most three terminal vertices, leaving six\ncolors for the private clique. For a common five-set we compress to the chromatic number of its original\nterminal graph. This yields nonuniform reductions with one extra neighbor\nper private vertex: all bipartite terminal graphs reduce, and three extra\nports at a dominant component handle a broad part of the five-cycle case.\nA separate finite surface lemma also handles any extra endpoint with private\nspokes in both connected layers; 1105 boundary states have independently\nchecked rotation certificates. The remaining one-extra configurations have\norder at least 25 in a minimum\ncounterexample. We also prove a connected-private-layer extension to seven\ncommon terminals. These are local obstructions to a minimum counterexample;\nno occurrence theorem or unrestricted nine-color theorem is claimed.",
